Codex Web GPT fork layer
========================

This directory holds everything redbean87/codex-chatgpt-web adds on top of
upstream (https://github.com/miuuyy/codex-chatgpt-web). Upstream code stays
byte-identical except for the registered hook blocks and patches below. This
file is plain text with no extension on purpose, mirroring the Firstmate fork.


Ownership boundary
------------------

  fork-owned    everything under fork/.
  hook file     an upstream file that carries registered hook blocks or a
                registered patch. Canonical copies of the blocks are
                fork/hooks/*.block; patches are fork/patches/*.patch. They are
                the only fork edits to upstream files.
  upstream      every other file. The fork never edits these.

The registry of hooks and patches is fork/lib/registry.ts.

Upstream files the fork touches, and why each touch is unavoidable:

  src/server.ts
    HK1-IMPORT  one import of fork/project-route.
    HK1         one call, in responseRequest right after traceId is assigned and
                before the cancelled-session check: forkRecordProjectRoute(
                traceId, req.headers). The server is the only process that sees
                the HTTP headers.
  src/adapters/chatgpt-web/browser-worker.ts
    HK2-IMPORT  one import of fork/project-route.
    HK2-SIG     one trailing optional parameter, routeKey, on prepareChatSurface.
    HK2         one block at the top of prepareChatSurface that asks the fork
                module to open the turn's project page. It returns the verified
                composer, or nothing, in which case upstream's own navigation and
                assertNewChatPage run unchanged.
    HK2-ARG-TURN, HK2-ARG-RETRY
                the two turn-bound call sites (new-turn preparation and the
                connector-catalog-stale retry) pass turn.traceId as routeKey.
                Every other caller omits it and is untouched.
  src/adapters/chatgpt-web/browser-worker.ts, optional patch
    HK3         fork/patches/code-fence-language.patch, independent of routing.
                See "Code-fence patch".

Nothing else changes: not package.json, not the request schema, not
src/chatgpt-session.ts (upstream's assertNewChatPage stays strict; the project
page is checked by the fork's own assertSurface).


Tools
-----

  fork/bin/fork-hook-check [--root <repo>] [--base <upstream-ref>]
      Proves the integration points are present, unmodified, and at their
      lifecycle positions. For every registered hook: exactly one begin and
      one end marker line in its file, enclosed text byte-identical to the
      canonical block, the block at its anchor, and the upstream names it uses
      still defined. For every registered patch: fully applied or cleanly
      applicable, never partial. No other src/ file may reference the fork.
      With --base, `git diff <ref>` outside fork/ may touch only the hook and
      patch files, and only by adding exactly the registered blocks (plus the
      patch hunks when a patch is applied). Prints one `ok:` or `FAIL:` line
      per check and exits 1 if any fails.

  fork/bin/fork-sync prepare <upstream-commit> [--from <branch>] [--worktree <dir>]
      Needs the commit already fetched (run `git fetch --no-tags upstream`
      yourself; the script never contacts a remote). Records <branch> (default
      main) as refs/backup/<branch>-pre-sync-<short>, creates branch
      fork/sync-<short> in a NEW worktree and merges the commit there with
      --no-ff. The active checkout, its branch, and its working tree are never
      touched. On conflicts the merge is left in progress in the worktree and
      every conflicted path is listed with its owner:
        fork-owned  upstream now writes a path the fork owns; stop and decide.
        hook file   take upstream's side, then re-insert the blocks from
                    fork/hooks/ and re-apply fork/patches/.
        upstream    the fork edited an upstream file outside its hooks: a
                    boundary violation; remove that edit.
      Exit 0 merged cleanly, 3 conflicts, 1 or 2 refused.

  fork/bin/fork-sync check <upstream-commit> [--root <dir>] [--from <branch>]
      In <root> (default this checkout): the boundary check with
      --base <upstream-commit>, a guard that the routing header name is defined
      in exactly one production file, the fork typecheck, `bun run verify`,
      `bun test ./fork/tests`, then the commits the import brings and the
      upstream-owned diffstat, for review. Exit 0 only if everything passes.

Nothing here pushes, fast-forwards, deletes a branch or worktree, aborts a
merge, or resolves a conflict; those steps stay manual.

Tests: `bun test ./fork/tests` (the repository's own `bun run test` runs only
./tests, and package.json is deliberately not edited).


Project routing
---------------

Goal: a consultation about repository X opens its ChatGPT chat inside the
ChatGPT project named X.

  caller (Firstmate)   adds the header  x-fm-chatgpt-project: <repo name>  to its
                       POST /v1/responses. The request body and endpoint are
                       unchanged. No header means exactly upstream behaviour.
  server (HK1)         validates the name (^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,63}$) and
                       writes <home>/fork-routes/<traceId>.json (mode 0600). The
                       trace id is the one the adapter computes for the same turn
                       (chatGptWebTraceId), so the server and the browser helper,
                       which are separate processes, agree on the file name.
                       Files older than 24 hours are pruned. Failures are logged
                       with a [fork] prefix and never fail the request.
  helper (HK2)         reads the route by turn.traceId, looks the exact,
                       case-sensitive name up in the project map, validates the
                       URL (^https://chatgpt\.com/g/g-p-[A-Za-z0-9-]+/project$) and
                       navigates there instead of https://chatgpt.com/. The page
                       must be on the configured origin and project path (a
                       canonical <id>-<slug> form of a full 32-hex id is accepted),
                       must not be a temporary chat, and must show the composer.

Fallback, always before Send: no route, an unmapped name, an absent or broken map,
or a navigation or surface failure (one retry, then give up) all continue with
upstream's ordinary new-chat preparation. The first project-or-fallback decision
per trace id is persisted and reused, so a retried preparation of the same turn
cannot switch surfaces. Nothing here runs after Send, so a fallback can never
replay a prompt or create a second conversation.

Outcome: <home>/fork-routes/<traceId>.outcome.json holds {project, decision:
"project"|"fallback", reason: "unmapped"|"navigation_failed", member?, at}. It
never contains a project URL. `member` is diagnostic evidence only: whether the
conversation URL reached after Send is inside the configured project. A mismatch
never fails a consultation.

Project map: a flat JSON object, name -> project URL, read from the first of
$FORK_PROJECTS_CONFIG, <home>/fork-projects.json, or fork/config/projects.local.json
(source checkouts). fork/config/projects.example.json shows the shape with
placeholder ids. <home> is $CODEX_CHATGPT_WEB_HOME or ~/.codex-chatgpt-web. The
browser helper is a bundled process: Bun inlines the source directory of
project-route.ts into the bundle, so a helper built from a checkout also reads that
checkout's fork/config/projects.local.json. A map outside the checkout (the first
two locations) survives a rebuild from elsewhere.

Unverified against live ChatGPT (an isolated acceptance test must settle these
before the running install switches to the fork): that a project URL of this shape
opens a page with the usual composer; that the first Send from it creates a
conversation inside the project (the `member` field records this); and that a
renamed or deleted project behaves as the fallback expects. If the observed URL
shape differs, change PROJECT_URL_PATTERN in fork/project-route.ts only.

Everything above is exercised by `bun test ./fork/tests` with fake pages, an
in-memory filesystem, and a loopback bridge on an ephemeral port; ChatGPT and the
running bridge are never contacted.


Code-fence patch (HK3)
----------------------

fork/patches/code-fence-language.patch keeps assistant-reply code fences labelled
(```json rather than a bare ```) when ChatGPT declares the language only as
data-language or as a single known toolbar label. Firstmate's plan validator
rejects an unlabelled fence. It is a separate delta from routing: it is applied in
its own commit, fork-hook-check verifies it as applied or cleanly applicable, and
it appears in no routing hook. Its regression tests and the three fixtures they
need live in fork/tests/code-fence-language.test.ts and fork/fixtures/; without the
patch applied those tests skip.


Private configuration
---------------------

fork/.gitignore keeps fork/config/projects.local.json (and any other
fork/config/*.local.json) out of git. The fork repository is public, so real
ChatGPT project URLs never go in a tracked file. fork/tests/privacy.test.ts fails
if a configured URL or a user-specific absolute path reaches a publishable file.
